# Raspberry Pi device provider

The provider broadcasts `AQUA_DISCOVER` on UDP port `12345`, collects
`AQUA_FOUND` responses from TimerNTP nodes, tracks the active device list, and
publishes retained JSON to MQTT topic `discovered/devices`.

## Dependencies and build

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y build-essential libmosquitto-dev libmosquitto1 \
  mosquitto-clients libcjson-dev

make
```

Network and MQTT defaults are compile-time constants in `provider.h`, including
the directed broadcast address, WireGuard broker address, MQTT port, discovery
interval, and topic. Review them before deployment.

## MQTT credentials

On first interactive start, the provider asks for the MQTT username and
password and stores them in `~/.mqtt_provider_auth` with mode `0600`. Run the
binary once as the same account configured in the service before enabling
unattended startup:

```bash
./provider
```

Stop it with `Ctrl+C` after the credentials file has been created.

## systemd service

Edit `mqtt-devices-provider.service` so `ExecStart`, `WorkingDirectory`, and
`User` match the installation. Then install and start it:

```bash
sudo cp mqtt-devices-provider.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now mqtt-devices-provider.service
sudo systemctl status mqtt-devices-provider.service
```

The service is ordered after both `network-online.target` and
`wg-quick@wg0.service`, because its default broker address is reached through
WireGuard.
